---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { RATING_WEIGHTS } from '../lib/rating';

const ownership = RATING_WEIGHTS.ownership;
const manufacturing = RATING_WEIGHTS.manufacturing;
const inputs = RATING_WEIGHTS.inputs;
const halfOwnership = ownership / 2;
const halfManufacturing = manufacturing / 2;
const halfInputs = inputs / 2;
---

<Base
  title="How we rate"
  description="The American rating is a sum of three sourced lines: who owns and runs the company, where it is made, and whether the inputs are American. Automation is shown and worth zero. The same page says how a record is written."
>
  <p class="crumb">
    <a href="/">Manufacturers</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
    <span>How we rate</span>
  </p>
  <div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">The number</p>
    <h1>How we rate</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A sum of three sourced lines, out of {ownership + manufacturing + inputs}. It is not a product review, and it does not sort the list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="weights">
    <h2 id="weights">The three lines that count</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Each line is full, half, none, or unknown. Full takes the whole weight. Half takes half. None is zero. Unknown is left out of the total, so a missing fact does not look like a failing grade.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="weight-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">Weights</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Line</th>
            <th scope="col">Full</th>
            <th scope="col">Half</th>
            <th scope="col">None</th>
            <th scope="col">Unknown</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Who owns and runs it</th>
            <td>{ownership}</td>
            <td>{halfOwnership}</td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>Left out</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Where it is made</th>
            <td>{manufacturing}</td>
            <td>{halfManufacturing}</td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>Left out</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Inputs from America</th>
            <td>{inputs}</td>
            <td>{halfInputs}</td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>Left out</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="means">
    <h2 id="means">What each line means</h2>
    <div class="questions" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">{ownership} points</p>
        <h3>Who owns and runs it</h3>
        <p>
          Full when the record is American-owned and American-run, and the confidence is “reported.” Half when that same code is only partly sourced. None when a foreign parent or foreign control is established. Unknown when the ownership code is unverified, or the confidence is unverified.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">{manufacturing} points</p>
        <h3>Where it is made</h3>
        <p>
          Full when the goods this record covers are made in the United States, reported. Half when that account is only partial, or when production is split across countries. None when the manufacturing is abroad. Unknown when the plant is unverified. A past chapter that the record says has ended stays in the change log. It does not by itself cut a current “made here” line in half.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">{inputs} points</p>
        <h3>Inputs from America</h3>
        <p>
          Full when every component comes from the United States and the confidence is “reported.” Half when the origins are mixed: some from the United States and some foreign, any component marked mixed, or a known origin beside an unknown one. Half also when every component comes from the United States and the confidence is only partial. None when every component is foreign. Unknown, and left out of the total, when inputs are left off the file, the code is unknown, the confidence is unverified, or every component origin is unknown.
        </p>
        <p>
          A scored inputs line is a component list. Each row names the part, its share, and its origin, and the shares total 100. The profile prints that list under Inputs from America, with the short note and its sources. When the sources do not give those shares, leave the field off or set the code to unknown. Do not invent a percentage.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="automation-score">
    <h2 id="automation-score">Automation is on the card and worth zero</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        The fourth line is automation. If the sources support a heavily automated shop, and the company is already American-owned and American-run, the breakdown says so. That line is worth 0. It does not add points. It does not take points off. The company stays in alphabetical order.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        If automation is not established, the line says “Not established” and the figure stays “Not in the score.” An empty field is not a penalty, and it is not a claim that the shop is all hand work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="not">
    <h2 id="not">What the number is not</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        It does not sort the home list or a category shelf. It does not measure whether the fork feels good in your hand. It does not stand in for a cap table, a mill certificate, or a floor audit. When a line is thin, the points follow the record that is actually there.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        <a href="#record">How a record is written</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="record" aria-labelledby="record-heading">
    <h2 id="record-heading">How a record is written</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:0.75rem">A file on each manufacturer. Two facts, kept apart.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-lines">
    <h2 id="two-lines">The two lines</h2>
    <div class="questions" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">01</p>
        <h3>Who owns and runs it</h3>
        <p>
          Who owns the company, and who runs it day to day. A plant in the United States with a foreign parent stays on the foreign-parent side of this line. The factory address does not change the owner.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">02</p>
        <h3>Where it is made</h3>
        <p>
          Where the manufacturing happens. A company owned in the United States and producing abroad is recorded as not made in the USA, or as split when the work really is in more than one place.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="filed">
    <h2 id="filed">What gets filed</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Each company is a JSON file in this site’s repository. The page is that file. Profiles are not kept in Netlify Blobs.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        A review or a proposed edit can be sent from a manufacturer page, or from <a href="/submit">the corrections page</a>. The note waits in a queue. It is not written into the file from the form, and it does not appear on the site until a person has checked it. An edit that holds up is applied later, in the repository.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The page stays on the manufacturer: owners, plant, dated changes, sources. Patterns and prices stay on the company’s own site.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        A made-in-USA directory can put a name on a lead list. It does not fill this one, and this site does not copy that directory’s filters or product grids.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        If a document is missing, or two accounts disagree, the profile says so. A fact that was not read is not written up as established.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="automation">
    <h2 id="automation">When a shop is heavily automated</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Some companies are American-founded, American-owned, and American-run, and the plant is also heavily automated. When the sources support that, the file can include a short note: a label, a sentence or two, and the source ids.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The note is a fact about the shop. It is not a warning. It does not move the company down the list or off it. The list on the front page is alphabetical.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        If the sources do not establish how automated the plant is, leave the field off. An empty field means not established. It does not mean someone verified a fully manual shop, and the file should not store <span class="path">heavilyAutomated: false</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Set the note only when the ownership line is already American-owned and American-run, and the write-up supports an American founding. A foreign-owned plant does not get this note. Using some machines is not enough. The sources have to support calling the shop heavily automated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="shelves">
    <h2 id="shelves">Shelves, then the file</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Categories are how a shopper browses: flatware now, and another shelf when a company is filed under it. The category page names the goods and the manufacturer. The profile is still the company, with the change log and the sources.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Three lines are scored, in the table above. Automation is the fourth line, shown, and worth zero.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
